Show the WiFi password dialog even when gnome-keyring is not installed.

Cinnamon's built-in NM secret agent (a gnome-shell fork, default since
Cinnamon 6.4 with nm-applet blacklisted from autostart) queries
org.freedesktop.secrets before prompting and aborts the request
(NM_SECRET_AGENT_ERROR_FAILED, no dialog) when the service is missing,
so activation fails without ever asking for the password. Treat the
"service unknown" D-Bus error on interactive requests as "no stored
secrets" so the dialog is shown; NetworkManager saves WPA-PSK secrets
system-owned (psk-flags=0) in /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections by
itself, so prompting, saving and reconnecting all work keyring-free.
Ports network-manager-applet 1.8.14 commit ecdf7087 ("applet-agent:
tolerate keyring not being there for interactive requests").

diff --git a/src/cinnamon-network-agent.c b/src/cinnamon-network-agent.c
--- a/src/cinnamon-network-agent.c
+++ b/src/cinnamon-network-agent.c
@@ -259,6 +259,17 @@
   self = closure->self;
   priv  = self->priv;
 
+  /* No secret service is available on the bus (e.g. gnome-keyring is not
+   * installed). If interaction is allowed, tolerate this and treat it as
+   * "no stored secrets" so the password dialog is still presented to the
+   * user; NetworkManager then stores system-owned secrets by itself.
+   * Mirrors network-manager-applet commit ecdf7087 ("applet-agent:
+   * tolerate keyring not being there for interactive requests").
+   */
+  if ((closure->flags & NM_SECRET_AGENT_GET_SECRETS_FLAG_ALLOW_INTERACTION) &&
+      g_error_matches (secret_error, G_DBUS_ERROR, G_DBUS_ERROR_SERVICE_UNKNOWN))
+    g_clear_error (&secret_error);
+
   if (secret_error != NULL)
     {
       g_set_error (&error,
